{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's distinguishing evidence ('Counter-Intuitive Correctness') conflates outcome-correctness with a genuine reliability mechanism, failing the problem's requirement to separate a true gain from 'mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer.' If the panel follows the checklist and the checklist's markers happen to be correlated with the verifiable outcome on trap cases, the panel will reach the correct answer by 'ignoring the intuitive lure' — but this is precisely compliance that tracks the right answer, not evidence the gain is genuine. The proposal has no within-evaluation test for whether the checklist is causally capturing the outcome-generative rule versus merely being correlated with it on the tested trap distribution. A checklist keyed to surface features that happen to co-occur with correct outcomes in the evaluation set would produce identical 'counter-intuitive correctness' without any genuine reliability gain.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_setup":"Administer two checklists to the non-expert panel: Checklist A (genuine) encodes the actual causal/verifiable rule that determines the outcome; Checklist B (spurious) encodes a surface heuristic that is correlated with the correct answer on the chosen trap cases but is causally irrelevant to the outcome.","result":"Both panels show counter-intuitive correctness on the evaluation traps (they resist the intuitive decoy and reach the right answer). But only Checklist A generalizes to holdout cases where the surface heuristic diverges from the true rule. SRC_001's criterion cannot distinguish A from B within the single evaluation."}],"target":"ad4ed64f921ce30b5daf4510ad5912e45b3730f1d782425ecfb0013e1c7dd266"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 fails to identify within-evaluation evidence that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its proposed mechanism — System 2 slowness preventing premature closure — is a process claim, but the problem asks for evidence *within a single evaluation* that the gain is genuine and not just compliance tracking correct outcomes. SRC_002 gives no observable, evaluatable criterion: 'slower processing' is not directly observable in outcome data, and even if processing time were measured, longer time is compatible with rote checklist-following that tracks the answer without genuine reliability. A panel could methodically and slowly apply a checklist whose steps merely co-occur with correct outcomes, producing System-2-style slowness and correct answers without any genuine gain. The proposal thus does not answer the distinguishing-evidence part of the question.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_setup":"Two panels both take longer (System 2) and both reach correct outcomes on the evaluation set. Panel G uses a checklist that enforces verification against the outcome-determining rule; Panel C uses a checklist whose steps are merely correlated with correct answers on this item set.","result":"Both exhibit the temporal/analytical signature SRC_002 cites as the mechanism, but only Panel G has a genuine reliability gain. SRC_002 offers no within-evaluation observable that separates them, since the proposed evidence is process-mode presence, not outcome-discriminating validity."}],"target":"bbdbaee5bd6ac20cdee20c2b94d57c8e5ccedfc83bebaa763b3e61a4b7d29bf8"}]}